# `total` is declared in front of a loop, and in that loop `forEach`, or a function of the program, is handed an arrow that adds to it.
# f1 = each, f2 = sum, f3 = sumWith. sum: the fast loops of `forEach` and the arrow are copied into the turn, and `total` comes round them and round the loop around as a parameter. Behind the fast loops stands the call of the rest of
# `forEach` with the arrow: in the loop around, on a way that is not rare. A scope is made there out of what the state holds, lent and read back, as where the call stands in no loop that the variable comes
# round. It was refused: the first scope was lent, the variable stayed in memory, and every turn of the fast loops read it, boxed it and stored it. The loop around is built twice: each call makes its scope.
# sumWith: `each` and the arrow are copied into the typed copy of the loop; its closed copy calls `each` and lent the first scope, so that the typed copy kept the store of every turn.
